<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Karnat dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1097 CE – 1324 CE (approx.) · capital Simraungadh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A dynasty of Karnataka origin that ruled Mithila from Simraungadh for two centuries and briefly held the Kathmandu Valley.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyadeva">Nanyadeva</a>, a chief of Karnata descent who probably came north with the armies of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Chalukyas of Kalyani</a>, established himself in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirhut">Tirhut</a> plain around 1097. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simraungadh">Simraungadh</a>, near the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a> border, his capital and ruled the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and the Himalayan foothills.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Karnat court was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Maithili learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harisimhadeva">Harisimhadeva</a>, the last king, sponsored the codification of Maithil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> genealogies (the panji system) and his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jyotirishvara-thakur">Jyotirishvara</a> wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna-ratnakara">Varna Ratnakara</a>, one of the earliest prose works in Maithili. The kings also intervened in the politics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, where some Karnat rulers were recognised as overlords.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1324 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-tughluq">Ghiyath al-Din Tughluq</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirhut">Tirhut</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simraungadh">Simraungadh</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harisimhadeva">Harisimhadeva</a> fled into the hills of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> soon passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oiniwar-dynasty">Oiniwar dynasty</a>, ruling as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, while Karnat traditions persisted in Nepalese court culture.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/karnat-dynasty.jpg" alt="Karnat dynasty" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shatkona_Seal_at_Simraungadh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1097 CE – 1324 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Simraungadh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nanyadeva, Harisimhadeva</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Maithili, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karnats of Mithila, Simraungadh kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1210&amp;focus=karnat-dynasty" aria-label="Karnat dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6921 1191 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Karnat dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7241 1447L7018 1385M7241 1447L7275 1464"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M7275 1464h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7241 1447h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7241 1447h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Simraungadh. <a href="/